Z||000000||dodger||06-12-2000||09:17 PM||||I know this might be a bit boring, but... I'mgetting random no start (3 times now).<BR>My 2.8 is my daly driver, and about once every week it won't start for about 5 0r ten minutes... it turns over and over but no catch at all. Pump the accelerator or not, no difference....no gas smell...just cranking over and over. Then like magic it fires right up. It does it afer driving awhile, but once after being stopped for 2 hours. Then only symptom is it does the usual bucking in 1st and second.<P>The problem is...how to take it in and find the problem BEFORE I get stuck!<BR>Thanks for any ideas...I read all the threads every day and know you guys could figure it out!||||reg||4
Z||000001||My7Fieros||06-12-2000||10:05 PM||||Try replacing the pick up coil and the igniton module...after getting warm they start acting up like that....pretty easy fix...<BR>Also might wanna check your starter..might be going bad||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||06-12-2000||10:55 PM||||Also check for the fuel pump running for 2 seconds when you first put the key to the run position.  Mine turned out to be a problem with the fuel pump relay AND the pickup coil having 160,000 miles worth of green, slimey corrosion.  Some guys change the pickup coil every time they do the plugs.  It's cheap.  Relay was around $10, and around $8 for the pickup coil and $1 for the distributor tube o-ring.  If you aren't already leaking oil, it will unless you don't replace that, too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||dodger||06-13-2000||01:16 AM||||Thanks guys...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||06-13-2000||03:32 AM||||I say starter, or since you got the same symptoms as I did, a bad (loose) starter cable.  Check them all!||||reg||1
Z||000005||84 Indy||06-13-2000||08:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84 Indy:<BR><B>I'd check the fusible link on the starter it happened to me.  The car would sometimes not start.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||3
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||06-14-2000||06:06 PM||||It can't be the starter. If the engine turns over (with decent force) the starter is fine. What Dodger was saying is that it's turning over, but it's not firing up. Well, I always try to break down the problem to figure it out. And engine needs Oxygen, Fuel, Spark and Compression to get going. Usually, compression isn't the problem, so, if you know you are getting air threw, and the EFI is working, then that only leaves spark. Spark comes from the plugs, plug wires, distributer cap, rotor, and pickup coil. Check the plugs, are the tips ugly and oily or a nice tan? are the plug wires in good condition? is the distributor cap cracked? is the rotor worn? is the coil still working? basically, an ingnition tune up, costs probrably $50 or less to replace everything listed, and shouldn't be much of a problem for a novice with some tools, time and a buddy w/ beer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||chris6878||06-14-2000||06:47 PM||||is the pick up coil the thing inside the dis cap that plugs up and has wires on it?  or is that the ign module...   my fiero doesnt want to start sometimes when warm either. It will just flood, then let it sit a while and cool.. starts up first time...||||reg||3
Z||000008||2birds||06-14-2000||08:01 PM||||The ignition module is a black plastic electronic device, held to the distributor base with two tiny crews.  <BR>The pickup coil is the coiled wire bundle that is wrapped around the distributor tube, under the reluctor wheel.  The pickup coil plugs into the ignition module on one side (two wires).  <BR>To replace the pickup coil, the distributor must be disassembled by removing the drive gear from the bottom, and pushing the drive shaft out the top (reluctor wheel is not removed).  <P>Check out the Fiero Online Service Guide for more info.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 06-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Linx||06-15-2000||10:03 PM||||Fellas, my '86 SE had the same problem big time!  And ya'll are gonna say "no way!", but it went away when i replaced my O2 sensor.  No question about it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ThatRickGuy||06-16-2000||09:22 AM||||Lynx, Good call, ya know, I had a buddy in highschool that had the same problem, I didn't even think of it until you mention it.||||reg||1
